For the implementation of the SA-CCR supervisory delta for options in your pricing library for an EU bank, you should use the following formula as per the regulatory requirements:

For call and put options that entitle the option buyer to purchase or sell an underlying instrument at a positive price on a single or multiple dates in the future, except where those options are mapped to the interest rate risk or commodity risk category, the supervisory delta is calculated as:

$$
\mathfrak{S} = \text{sign} \cdot N\left(\text{type} \cdot \frac{\ln \left(\frac{P}{K}\right) + 0.5 \cdot \sigma^{2} \cdot T}{\sigma \cdot \sqrt{T}}\right)
$$

Where:
- $\mathfrak{S}$ = the supervisory delta;
- $\text{sign} = -1$ where the transaction is a sold call option or a bought put option;
- $\text{sign} = +1$ where the transaction is a bought call option or sold put option;
- $\text{type} = -1$ where the transaction is a put option;
- $\text{type} = +1$ where the transaction is a call option;
- $N(x)$ = the cumulative distribution function for a standard normal random variable;
- $P$ = the spot or forward price of the underlying instrument of the option;
- $K$ = the strike price of the option;
- $T$ = the expiry date of the option, expressed in years using the relevant business day convention;
- $\sigma$ = the supervisory volatility of the option.

This formula is specified in Article 279a(1) of the CRR (Regulation 575/2013) and is applicable for options that are not mapped to the interest rate risk or commodity risk categories. For options mapped to these categories, a different formula is used, which includes a shift ($\lambda$) to ensure that both the spot or forward price ($P$) and the strike price ($K$) are in positive territory, as detailed in Article 5(1) of the Delegated Regulation 2021/931.

Please ensure that you also consider the supervisory volatility ($\sigma$) for the specific risk category of the underlying instrument, as outlined in the regulatory documents.
